<Project>

  <!--
    One place every package version lives. `Temporalio` was declared twice by hand
    at the same version, which is the kind of agreement that survives exactly until
    one of the two is edited. A project then says which packages it uses and
    `Directory.Packages.props` says which versions they are.
  -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <ManagePackageVersionsCentrally>true</ManagePackageVersionsCentrally>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup Label="The factory">
    <!--
      The one package two of the three libraries take — `Application` for
      `WorkItemScope`/`ProjectScope` and `Infrastructure` for the result deriver — and the
      reason a record can be written from either: `ILogger<T>` is how that is expressed.
      `Microsoft.NET.Sdk` and the Web SDK carry it implicitly; the libraries carry nothing,
      so it is declared. It is the abstractions and not the whole logging stack, because a
      scope opens a record and does not choose where the record goes — which is #63's, on
      the far side of the boundary. `Domain` takes nothing, ever.
    -->
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Logging.Abstractions" Version="10.0.13" />
    <!--
      `FactoryDriver` is the heartbeat: a `BackgroundService` whose whole body is one
      `Task.Delay` and two calls into the policy core. It is the only hosted service in
      `Application`, and `BackgroundService`/`IHostedService` live here. It is the same
      kind of package as the logging abstractions — the framework's own contract for being
      run and stopped, carrying no adapter and reaching nothing outside — not a transport,
      a disk, a process or a credential.
    -->
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Hosting.Abstractions" Version="10.0.13" />
    <!--
      `TemporalTickets` resolves the store, the policy core and its own logger from the
      container as it hosts the worker, so `Infrastructure` names `IServiceProvider`'s own
      extension. The Web SDK carries this package implicitly and `Microsoft.NET.Sdk` does
      not, so it is declared rather than assumed — the same kind of reference as the
      hosting abstractions above, and declared with it for the same reason.
    -->
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection.Abstractions" Version="10.0.13" />
    <!--
      `FactoryPaths` and `FactorySettings` read a configuration key before they resolve
      anything on a disk, so this project names `IConfiguration` and declares the
      abstractions for it. `Application` does not: `FactoryOptions` is the values, and
      nothing in the policy reads a key.
    -->
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Configuration.Abstractions" Version="10.0.13" />
    <!--
      The provider and the store's own database access, all in `Infrastructure` now that
      the store has crossed the boundary (#77): `AgentFactory.Web` names no database at all
      any more. It is at the 10.0.13 patch line with the rest, because EF brings
      `Microsoft.Data.Sqlite.Core` 10.0.13 transitively and a direct reference left behind
      would split the package across two versions.
    -->
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.Data.Sqlite" Version="10.0.13" />
    <!--
      The store of record over EF Core, on SQLite. `Infrastructure` owns the model — the
      `DbContext`, its row types and every mapping — and `Application` names none of it:
      `IWorkItemStore` is still a port, and this is the adapter behind it (#56, #62).
      **EF 10.0.13 requires the `Microsoft.Extensions.*` abstractions at 10.0.13**, so the
      four above move with it in the same change: holding them at 10.0.12 is a package
      downgrade, which this repository's `TreatWarningsAsErrors` refuses as NU1605 rather
      than letting it pass as a warning.
    -->
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Sqlite" Version="10.0.13" />
    <!--
      Required only by `dotnet ef`, which is the tool that generates the baseline
      migration, so it is a `PrivateAssets=all` reference in `Infrastructure` and travels
      nowhere. It is pinned to the same version as the provider because EF's tooling checks
      the tool and the runtime versions against each other.
    -->
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Design" Version="10.0.13" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Temporalio" Version="1.20.0" />
    <!--
      The Board's generated types come from the document this package's generator
      produces, and the generator is what turns the endpoints' own metadata — the
      `.Produces<T>()` annotations in `FactoryApp` — into the schemas
      `ClientApp/lib` is aliased over (#78, #80). It registers the generator and nothing
      else: `MapOpenApi()` is deliberately never called, so the process serves no
      document endpoint and the document is a build artefact read by the
      generation script in `ClientApp/scripts/`.
    -->
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.AspNetCore.OpenApi" Version="10.0.13" />
    <!--
      The build-time half of the pair above: this is what runs the document
      generator. It launches the application's entry point to do it, which is why
      `AgentFactory.Web.csproj` turns generation off on `Build` and the generation
      script invokes the target itself with the store pointed at a temporary path
     .
    -->
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.Extensions.ApiDescription.Server" Version="10.0.13" />
    <PackageVersion Include="YamlDotNet" Version="18.1.0" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    **The telemetry pipeline (#63), and the whole of it is `Web`'s.** `AddFactoryServiceDefaults`
    is the one caller (`Composition/ServiceDefaults`), and the components behind these are the
    process's outermost layer: they turn the factory's instruments, the framework's own and
    the ASP.NET Core pipeline's into OTLP and hand them to the dashboard the AppHost starts.

    **Every one of them is pinned to 1.20.0, and the research's `1.17.0` is superseded.**
    `docs/research/aspire.md` read 1.17.0 as current on the same day this was written; the
    registry's own index (`api.nuget.org/v3-flatcontainer/…`) answers 1.18.0, 1.19.0, 1.19.1
    and **1.20.0** for every package below, so the version this repository pins is the one the
    registry serves rather than the one the document recorded. The Aspire half of that research
    (`Aspire.AppHost.Sdk` 13.6.1) is unaffected and still current.

    OpenTelemetry versions on its own train — 1.x, independent of Aspire's 13.x and of
    `Microsoft.Extensions.*`'s 10.x — and every package's `Microsoft.Extensions.*` floor is
    10.0.0, below the 10.0.13 EF Core forced, so nothing here is a package downgrade
    (`NU1605`).

    **Two packages the Aspire service-defaults template carries are deliberately absent, and
    both absences are the point of this ticket.** `Microsoft.Extensions.Http.Resilience` is the
    standard resilience handler — `AddServiceDefaults()` puts it on *every* `HttpClient`,
    including the GitHub one, where its 10-second attempt timeout throws a
    `TimeoutRejectedException` that `GitHubClient` does not catch and which would therefore
    escape the seam unclassified (see `Composition/ServiceDefaults` and the pins in
    `PolicyTests`/`TheProcessTests`). `Microsoft.Extensions.ServiceDiscovery` resolves a
    service *name* to an address, and nothing in this factory names a service: GitHub is an
    absolute URL the project file states, and Temporal's address arrives as `host:port` from
    the AppHost. A dependency that resolves nothing is a dependency nobody is reviewing.
  -->
  <ItemGroup Label="The telemetry pipeline">
    <!-- `AddOpenTelemetry()`, the hosted service that builds the providers, and the provider builders. -->
    <PackageVersion Include="OpenTelemetry.Extensions.Hosting" Version="1.20.0" />
    <!-- The OTLP exporter: the one thing here that reaches outside the process. -->
    <PackageVersion Include="OpenTelemetry.Exporter.OpenTelemetryProtocol" Version="1.20.0" />
    <!--
      The SDK itself, declared by `Web` for `AddOpenTelemetry` and by the test project for the
      reader a test watches the pipeline with. Declared once here for both.
    -->
    <PackageVersion Include="OpenTelemetry" Version="1.20.0" />
    <!-- The factory's own request handling: server spans and `http.server.*` metrics for the board. -->
    <PackageVersion Include="OpenTelemetry.Instrumentation.AspNetCore" Version="1.20.0" />
    <!-- The factory's own outbound requests, which is the GitHub client and nothing else. -->
    <PackageVersion Include="OpenTelemetry.Instrumentation.Http" Version="1.20.0" />
    <!-- GC, thread pool, exceptions: what the process costs, which no other signal reports. -->
    <PackageVersion Include="OpenTelemetry.Instrumentation.Runtime" Version="1.20.0" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    **The AppHost's packages, and one forced upgrade (#63).** Aspire arrives as an MSBuild
    SDK rather than as a `PackageReference` (`src/AgentFactory.AppHost`), so it has no
    version to pin here — see that project's own comment for the one place the version
    lives. What *does* live here is the one package its dependency graph pulls in below a
    patched version.
  -->
  <ItemGroup Label="The AppHost">
    <!--
      **A transitive package of Aspire's orchestration machinery, lifted to the patched
      release, and the reason is `TreatWarningsAsErrors` telling the truth.** Aspire 13.6.1's
      graph resolves `MessagePack` 2.5.302, which `NU1902` reports against
      GHSA-qhrr-8q5h-9q3h/CVE-2026-92707 — and this repository's build treats a NuGet audit
      warning as an error, so the AppHost does not build until the version is raised.
      `2.5.303` is the patched release on the same line Aspire depends on, which is why the
      fix is a bump here rather than a `NoWarn` on the project: suppressing the audit would
      be the repository saying a known vulnerability in its own dependency graph is
      acceptable, and nothing here has decided that. `MessagePack` is not reachable from any
      factory code — it is Aspire's, on the other side of the host boundary — and it is not
      referenced *by* anything here either: the version is applied through the AppHost's
      central transitive pinning, so the fix costs no unreviewed direct reference.
    -->
    <PackageVersion Include="MessagePack" Version="2.5.303" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup Label="The suite">
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.NET.Test.Sdk" Version="17.14.1" />
    <PackageVersion Include="xunit" Version="2.9.3" />
    <PackageVersion Include="xunit.runner.visualstudio" Version="3.1.4" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    `Testcontainers` was declared by the test project and used by nothing. It drove
    the worker-image tests, which the rounds-run-on-the-host rule deleted along with the worker image, and
    the only mention left in the suite is the assertion in `RoundTests` that the
    factory takes no container-runtime package at all — which the reference was
    contradicting. It goes with the ADR that stranded it.
  -->

</Project>